const GLOSS={
  origin:['Origin Story','Create a basic hero over six comic pages: life before the mask, the incident (sets the discipline), the power, a fighting style, a motivation, and the mask. Each choice shows its exact effect. Basic heroes are free, start at level 1 with no gear and change nothing at HQ.'],
  crossover:['Crossover Issue','From Arc 2, once per arc, recruit an Established Character. They go through the same origin pages plus pages about their own series. They arrive at level arc + 1 (arc + 2 with a long-running title), with extra discipline ranks, signature upgrades, gear from your unlocked blueprints and a bonus for HQ.'],
  quickbuild:['Quick builds','The nine classic Guardians, ready made. They follow the basic hero rules: level 1 and no gear, but each is a veteran with +1 to their discipline: +1 max Power, +2 max health or +1 max Arcana. Each can be on the roster once.'],
  established:['Established Character','Recruited through a Crossover Issue. They joined at a higher level, with their own gear and a bonus for HQ.'],
  hp:['Health','Damage lowers health. At 0 the hero is knocked out of the fight, and is back at half health after the mission.'],
  move:['Movement','How many hexes the hero can move each round, one hex at a time. Heroes cannot pass through other units, civilians or rubble.'],
  speed:['Attack dice','How many d20s the hero rolls per attack. Each die that reaches the target\'s evasion (after accuracy) is a hit. A natural 20 always hits, a natural 1 always misses.'],
  acc:['Accuracy','Added to every attack die.'],
  str:['Strength','Added to the d20 wound roll. A wound roll must reach the villain\'s toughness to tear a page from its playbook.'],
  eva:['Evasion','Enemies roll a d20 plus their accuracy and must reach this number to hit the hero.'],
  range:['Range','How many hexes away the hero can attack. Range 1 means adjacent hexes only.'],
  comp:['Composure','The blue bar under each hero\'s health. The hero\'s nerve. Taunts and mind tricks drain it, 1 more from villains of level 3 and up and 2 more from level 6. At 0 the hero is Shaken: −3 Strength, −3 accuracy, −3 evasion and −1 movement until composure is restored.'],
  xp:['Experience','Each level costs one more XP than the last: 3, then 4, then 5. Heroes earn 1 XP per mission, +1 for landing 2 or more wounds, and +1 with a Training Room. Heroes recruited after the first issue start with 1 XP for every issue already completed.'],
  tech:['Tech discipline','Gadgets and engineering. Tech abilities spend Power: max Power is your Tech rank + 1 for each Tech item you wear, plus bonuses from your origin and gear. It starts full and slowly recharges: 1 Power each round, more with recharge bonuses. Every Tech item has an ability. Each ability and power can be used once per round. Ranks give no stats by themselves. Rank 5 is the capstone: +1 Power recharge.'],
  innate:['Innate discipline','Natural powers pushed further. Innate abilities spend nothing. Instead each requires a minimum Innate rank, and a hero can fire only one Innate ability per round. Many grow stronger with your Innate rank. Ranks give no stats by themselves. Rank 5 is the capstone: you can’t be Shaken, and once per fight you survive a blow that would knock you out with 1 health.'],
  magic:['Magic discipline','Spells, relics and wards. Magic spells spend Arcana: max Arcana is your Magic rank + the Magic rank each item you wear requires, plus bonuses. It starts full each fight and does not refill on its own, so spells hit harder than Tech abilities. Each spell and power can be cast once per round. Every Magic weapon carries a spell; most other Magic items give passive bonuses instead. Ranks give no stats by themselves. Rank 5 is the capstone: any page you tear staggers a charging Signature Move at once.'],
  'res:tech':['Power','Tech pool. Max = Tech rank + 1 per Tech item worn + bonuses. Full at the start of each fight, then slowly recharges: 1 per round plus recharge bonuses. Spent on Tech gear abilities.'],
  'res:innate':['Innate rank','Innate threshold, not a pool. An Innate ability works only if your Innate rank meets its number, many grow with it, and each hero can fire one Innate ability per round.'],
  'res:magic':['Arcana','Magic pool. Max = Magic rank + the Magic rank each worn item requires + bonuses. Full at the start of each fight and it does not refill each round. Only a few things restore it: Circuit Siphon, the Bloodstone, the Philosopher\'s Stone, Martyr\'s Vestments, the Guardian Locket and Magic rank 5.'],
  slot:['Gear slots','Head, Body, Main Hand, Off Hand, Legs, two Accessories and a Belt. Each slot holds one item. Main Hand weapons hit hardest; Off Hand items are defensive or lighter. Paired weapons go in either hand. A two-handed weapon fills both hands. Weapon and Body gear is the strongest and costs the most, then Head and Legs, then Accessories and Belts. Weapons carry most of the attack stats. Consumables go in a separate pouch of 2. Equipping needs the listed discipline ranks. Every Magic weapon carries a spell.'],
  paired:['Paired weapons','Some Tech and Innate weapons are Paired: they go in either hand and have off-hand stats. Hold the same weapon in both hands and both count in full (stats and set count), plus the pair bonus. Its ability is still one button. Craft it twice to pair it.'],
  blinded:['Blinded','The villain has −3 accuracy until the end of the round.'],
  excise:['Find the Flaw','The next page torn from this villain this round is one you choose from the top of its playbook, so you can rip out a Signature Move or its worst page.'],
  riposte:['Riposte','The first time the villain misses this hero this round, the hero strikes back with a free 1-die attack, if the villain is within their range.'],
  relent:['Relentless','Attacking the same villain round after round keeps up the pressure: +1 die if you also attacked it last round. Skip a round and it resets.'],
  aura:['Aura','Gear that helps the wearer and every ally within 2 hexes. Some auras add stats, and stat auras from different heroes add up. Others only work under a condition: healing or composure while at half or less, extra Power while nearly empty, less damage from big hits, or the first attack on you each round rolling twice and keeping the lower die.'],
  shaken:['Shaken','A hero with 0 composure. They get −3 Strength, −3 accuracy, −3 evasion and −1 movement (minimum 1) until composure is restored.'],
  dazed:['Dazed','The hero cannot move next round. They can still act.'],
  windward:['Wind Ward','The next attack against this hero, from the villain or a henchman, gets the listed evasion bonus. It is used up by that attack, hit or miss, and lasts until then.'],
  guard:['Guard','All damage-reduction effects combined (Ground Out, Brace Field, Load-Bearing, Shield Wall and other Guard effects) can reduce each hit by at most 1 at total discipline levels 1–2, 2 at levels 3–4, and 3 at level 5+. Guard never reduces a positive hit below 1 damage. Absorption shields and Wards are separate from this limit.'],
  absorb:['Absorb shield','From Arcane Aegis or Carapace. Absorbs the listed amount of damage from incoming hits, after any guard reduction, then fades. It lasts until used up or the fight ends.'],
  warded:['Warded','The next hit against this hero is turned aside completely.'],
  braced:['Braced','Until the next hero turn, villain attacks on adjacent allies hit this hero instead, for 1 less damage (never below 1).'],
  hexed:['Hexed','The villain has −4 evasion until the end of the round.'],
  boost:['Bonus movement','This hero\'s next move goes farther. If it says jump, that move can also clear one lit beam hex. It lasts until the hero moves, even into the next round.'],
  jumps:['All moves are jumps','Every move this hero makes is a jump, so it can clear one lit beam hex for 2 movement.'],
  set:['Villain set','Gear crafted from one villain\'s salvage belongs to that villain\'s set. Wear 4 pieces of the same set at once to turn on its set bonus. The hero card shows each set you are wearing just below your gear.'],
  buff:['Charged attack','A bonus from gear that applies to this hero\'s next attack, then is used up. Unused charges fade at the end of the round.'],
  trust:['Public trust','How much Blackharbor believes in the team. Saving civilians raises it. Harmed civilians, failures and villain schemes lower it. At 0 the team disbands.'],
  techres:['Tech','Spare parts and hardware. Spent on base facilities and research. Gear and consumables cost only salvage.'],
  intel:['Intel','Information networks and files. Spent on facilities, research and investigation options.'],
  salvage:['Villain salvage','Coils from Dr. Gallows, Shards from Mother Quarry, Ledgers from The Cantor, Rebar from The Architect, Sunstone from Helios Prime, Scales from Ouroboros, Lodestones from Lodestone, Umbra from Nocturne, Chitin from Murmur and Fuses from Payload. Earned by winning lair showdowns and used to craft gear.'],
  scheme:['Scheme','Each issue you don\'t hunt a villain, their scheme advances. When it fills, they strike the city: −2 trust and they gain a level.'],
  vlevel:['Villain level','Each level adds 3 new, stronger pages to the playbook. Every level adds +1 evasion, toughness and accuracy, and every 3 add +1 damage. A villain beaten in their lair escapes and comes back 1 level higher.'],
  playbook:['Playbook','The villain\'s deck of moves, and also its health. The next action sits in a separate charging slot. Normal wounds tear the draw pile. A signature is torn instead by the wound that reaches half its level plus 1 (rounded up) in one hero round. A final ordinary charging page can be torn when no other pages remain. When no pages remain, it is defeated.'],
  discard:['Discard','Pages the villain has already played this fight. They are shuffled back in when the playbook runs out.'],
  wound:['Wounds','Pages torn from the playbook this fight. The villain can no longer use them.'],
  facing:['Facing','The yellow arrow shows which way the villain faces. It turns toward whatever it last moved toward or attacked. Attacks from the blind spot behind it get +1 die.'],
  blind:['Blind spot','The narrow cone behind a villain, shaded on the board. Attacks from a blind-spot hex get +1 die. The villain turns as it moves and attacks, so the blind spot moves too.'],
  facearc:['Arcs','Every villain has a front arc (the way it faces), two side arcs, and a rear arc (the shaded blind spot). Attacks from the rear get +1 die. Some signature hit reactions only trigger in certain arcs, so read the playbook and pick your angle: some villains punish a head-on attack, others guard their back.'],
  vhide:['Hidden facing','Nocturne hides which way she faces. Her arrow shows a question mark and her arcs are not shaded, but they still count. Every hit you land reveals her facing until she fades again, and her Spotlight reveals it too.'],
  swarm:['Swarm','Murmur is three separate 1-hex bodies: a queen, a stinger and a shell. Each has its own facing, and you strike the body you aim at, so arcs are measured from that body. A body with another body right beside it gets +2 toughness. Each body carries one hit reaction that fires only when you strike that body. Tear the page it hangs on and the body falls out of the swarm. Recover the page and it re-forms.'],
  vsize:['Villain size','Villains fill 1, 4 or 7 hexes. Ouroboros is a 5-hex serpent whose body follows its head, and it grows one hex longer for every civilian or henchman it swallows. Murmur is a swarm of three separate 1-hex bodies. Each villain also has a size from 1 to 4 (1 for one hex, 3 for four hexes, 4 for five or more), shown in its case file: Ground Quake only shoves a villain whose size is no more than the hero\'s Innate rank. Anything bigger than one hex shoves heroes, henchmen and civilians out of its path when it moves. Some moves deal damage to whoever is shoved.'],
  sigmove:['Signature Move','A villain\'s biggest, most dangerous pages, marked in black and gold. When one is next in the playbook, the villain shouts a hint at the end of its turn. Its page moves out of the deck into the charging slot. Accumulate wounds equal to half the boss level plus 1 (rounded up) in one hero round: the threshold wound tears the signature instead of the top draw-pile page.'],
  hitdeck:['Hit deck','Every hit on the villain draws a card that changes the wound roll: weak points, armor, counterattacks and more. Campaign bosses use 5 unique reactions and 5 basic reaction cards; reactions linked to torn pages leave the deck.'],
  reaction:['Hit reactions','Signature cards each villain adds to the hit deck: armor, counters that punish adjacent attackers, mind tricks and escapes. Uncover them with clues, or by drawing them in a fight. Some reactions live on a playbook page: tear that page out and its linked reaction leaves the hit deck. Known ones appear at the back of the playbook.'],
  hints:['Style hints','What informants and old case files say about how this villain fights. Read them before choosing your party and gear.'],
  intelfile:['Intel','Each clue you find opens one playbook page or hit reaction you haven\'t seen yet. What you learn is kept for the rest of the volume and the next. Losing a clue does not take the intel back. Every clue past 4 also gives +1 intel. Long-press a card to read it.'],
  gearup:['Surprise gear-up','With 4 or more clues you catch the villain by surprise and can change your party\'s gear right before the showdown.'],
  clues:['Clues','Gathered while investigating. 0–1 means an ambush, 2–3 a standoff, 4 or more means you arrive prepared and can change gear first. Each new clue also reveals one playbook page or hit reaction.'],
  q_ambush:['Ambush','The villain acts first, with an extra henchman, and your heroes start scattered.'],
  q_normal:['Standoff','Heroes enter from the south edge and act first.'],
  q_prepared:['Prepared','You can change gear before entering, you act first, and the villain starts with 1 page already torn out. +1 salvage on a win.'],
  civilian:['Civilians','Bystanders caught in the fight. Escort them to an exit for +1 trust each. Villain attacks can harm them, which costs trust. On the villain\'s turn, a civilian with no hero beside them (marked !) panics and runs 1 hex from the nearest threat, maybe into a hazard. A henchman next to a loose civilian grabs them and drags them toward its villain until you knock it out. Civilians still on the board at the end cost nothing.'],
  exit:['Exits','Green hexes on the edge of the board. A civilian who reaches one is saved, and a hero who steps on one sends every civilian following them out.'],
  henchman:['Henchmen','Most regular minions start with 2 HP; Cantor\'s hired guns have only 1 HP. Regular henchmen can move up to 3 hexes, then attack within their type\'s range (Cantor and Gallows: 2; some others: 1 or 3). Damage starts at 1 and gains +1 every 4 villain levels. The number on each miniature shows remaining HP.'],
  elite:['Special henchman','A tougher lieutenant with a yellow border, brought by some villains from level 3. Health 2 + the villain level, harder to hit, +4 to hit you, and 1 + half the villain level in damage. Taking one down is worth 1 of that villain\'s salvage. The Cantor\'s Made Man is his bodyguard: while the elite stands beside him, it intercepts hero attacks aimed at him. Dr. Gallows\' Failed Experiment moves only 2, hits 1 harder and shrugs off the first hit each round. Ouroboros\' High Priest attacks from range 3 and raises a fallen Cultist each round while fewer than 2 remain. The Architect\'s Foreman bolts a girder next to the hero closest to the Architect each round: it is a wall that blocks shots (attacks from 2+ hexes away whose line crosses it), and after 2 villain turns it topples onto an adjacent hero for 2 + half the Architect\'s level. Payload\'s Bombardier moves 2, shoots from range 3, and on alternating turns replaces its shot with a range-4 bomb toss; its bombs explode next villain turn. Payload\'s henchmen are immune to her bombs.'],
  rubble:['Rubble','Blocks movement for heroes, henchmen and the villain.'],
  'act:move':['Move','Move once per round, up to your movement in straight steps. Until the hero takes their action you can tap any other blue hex to change where they end up, or their starting hex to take the move back. If they acted first, they can keep adjusting until the round ends.'],
  'act:attack':['Attack','Uses your action. Roll your attack dice against a target in range. Each hit on the villain draws from the hit deck and rolls to wound.'],
  walls:['Walls','Block movement and beams like rubble. Walls built into the lair stay for the whole fight; walls the villain raises crumble after a few rounds. Breach smashes them, and a villain bigger than one hex smashes straight through walls and rubble when it moves.'],
  pit:['Pit','A hole in the floor. Nobody can walk into one, but shots and beams pass over it. A hero who is jumping can clear one pit hex for 2 movement. Villains bigger than one hex stride right across.'],
  bomb:['Bombs','Payload\'s bombs always have a 1-round fuse and explode at the start of the next villain turn. Normal bombs cover 7 hexes (radius 1); certain advanced bombs expand to 19 hexes (radius 2) as Payload levels up. Red stripes show the full blast area. Bombs harm heroes, civilians, and non-Payload henchmen, but not Payload or her own Sappers and Bombardier. A Sapper next to a bomb adds 1 damage. At most 4 bombs can be lit at once.'],
  haz:['Molten floor','Hazard hexes. Any hero standing on one when the villain starts its turn takes the listed damage. Move off before ending the round.'],
  charge:['Charged attack','A telegraphed move. The marked hexes get hit on the villain\'s next turn, in place of a new page. Move out, or tear pages equal to half the villain\'s level plus 1 (rounded up) in one hero round to tear the charging page. Eclipse cancels it outright.'],
  vlead:['Villain trail','The third lead of every investigation is always one of the villain\'s own leads, built around how they operate.'],
  blueprint:['Blueprints','Facilities and gear start locked. Gear blueprints of tier 0 and 1 are open from the start, tier 2 opens in Arc 2, tier 3 at issue 10 (halfway through Arc 2), and tier 4 and 5 in Arc 3 (tier is the total rank an item needs). Some facilities open in later Arcs or by beating specific villains in their lairs. Skunkworks research and special investigation leads can get blueprints early.'],
  party:['Active party','Up to 4 heroes go on each mission. Change the party in the roster at HQ. Benched heroes recover fully and gain 1 XP every issue.'],
  field:['Field ability','A hero ability that works outside combat, during investigations or at the start of each issue, as long as the hero is in the active party.'],
  fieldgear:['Out-of-combat gear','Some gear helps outside fights: an extra clue, better rolls, healing, salvage or trust. It only works while the wearer is in the active party.'],
  marked:['Marked','Every hero attack against this villain from a side arc or the blind spot gets +1 die until the round ends. Each hero can mark once per fight.'],
  cursed:['Cursed','This villain deals 2 less damage with its attacks until the round ends, but every hit still deals at least 1.'],
  pouch:['Pouch and consumables','Each hero carries up to 2 consumables in a pouch, separate from their 8 gear slots. Consumables cost 1 or 2 salvage, need no Workshop and no discipline ranks, and are gone after one use. In a fight they are free actions. Some are used during investigations instead.'],
  capstone:['Capstone','Discipline ranks give no stats by themselves: they grow the engine and open higher gear. Reaching rank 5 in a discipline grants its capstone. Tech: +1 Power recharge. Innate: can’t be Shaken, and once per fight survive a blow that would knock you out with 1 health. Magic: any page you tear staggers a charging Signature Move at once, instead of needing the villain\'s level + 1 torn pages.'],
  bridge:['Bridge perks','Hybrid heroes unlock a bridge perk for every pair of disciplines they have at rank 2 or higher, and Paragon for rank 2 in all three. Technomancer (Tech + Magic): once per round, trade 3 Power for 1 Arcana or 1 Arcana for 3 Power. Augmented (Tech + Innate): once per round, when you take damage, gain that much Power. Primal (Innate + Magic): with Blood Price on, Magic abilities and powers cost 1 less Arcana and 1 composure. Paragon (all three): Tech and Magic abilities and powers have no once-per-round limit (you still pay for each use).'],
  hybrid:['Hybrid gear','Gear that needs ranks in two or three disciplines at once. Its ability follows the rules of its main discipline, shown by its color: Power for Tech, Arcana for Magic, Innate rank for Innate.'],
  volume:['Volume','A full campaign: 3 arcs of 6 issues, then an event comic finale where two villains attack together. Starting villains are random. At the end of each arc, active villains level up and upgrade their playbooks, and a new villain appears.'],
  arc:['Arc','Six issues. When an arc ends, every active villain gains a level and new pages, 3 old pages are replaced with upgraded versions, and a new villain arrives at level 2× the new arc number, or 1 above the strongest active villain if that is higher. The tip line also pays +1 intel for every 10 trust.'],
  engines:['Discipline engines','Tech runs on Power (slowly recharges), Magic on Arcana (one pool per fight, bigger spells), Innate on your Innate rank (free abilities above a threshold, one per round). Signature powers run on the engine of their discipline too. A hero only has the engines of disciplines they have ranks in.'],
  guard:['Guard','Until the round ends, every hit against this hero deals less damage (never below 1), or misses more often with bonus evasion. Absorb shields are the exception: they can soak a hit completely.'],
  anchor:['Anchored','This hero cannot be shoved, flung or dragged until the round ends.'],
  frozen:['Time Stop','The villain loses its next turn. Its next page, or its charged attack, is thrown away unplayed.'],
  callout:['Called out','The next hero attack against this villain this round gets the bonus dice.'],
  'act:escort':['Escort','Uses your action. Every civilian next to you starts following you, trailing behind as you move. Step on an exit to get them all out for +1 trust each. Attacking lets them go. Civilians held by a henchman can\'t be escorted until it is knocked out.'],
  'act:hack':['Hack','Uses your action. Stand on or next to the evidence terminal to download it for +2 intel. It can only be hacked once.'],
  terminal:['Evidence terminal','Every lair without a special henchman keeps a terminal on the far edge, behind the villain. A hero on it or beside it can Hack it with their action for +2 intel. Charged attacks, bombs and shockwaves that cover its hex destroy it first.'],
  overflow:['Overflow clues','You only need 4 clues to catch the villain by surprise. Every clue past 4 also gives +1 intel.'],
  'act:letgo':['Let go','Free. The civilians following you stop and stay where they are.'],
  evac:['Evacuated and harmed','Evac counts civilians you got out through an exit this fight, +1 trust each. Harmed counts civilians hurt by the villain or its henchmen, −1 trust each. Both are paid out in the aftermath.'],
  pips:['Playbook pages','One square per page. Solid squares are pages still in the playbook, faded ones are in the discard pile and come back when the playbook is reshuffled, and crossed-out ones are torn out for good. Tear out every page to win.'],
  levelup:['Level up','This hero has enough XP for a new level. Open their card in the roster: pick a discipline to grow, then one upgrade.'],
  beam:['Lit beam','Gridlock\'s beams stay lit across the board. A hero who steps into a lit hex takes the damage shown and their move ends there. Rubble and walls block beams.'],
  facility:['Facilities','Built once with tech and intel, then they work for the rest of the volume. Long-press one for details.'],
};

